# Comparison of Three Sorting Algorithms: Bubble Sort, Merge Sort, and Quick Sort

## Overview

| Algorithm | Average Time | Worst Time | Best Time | Space Complexity | Stable? | In-Place? |
|-----------|-------------|------------|-----------|-----------------|---------|-----------|
| Bubble Sort | O(n²) | O(n²) | O(n) | O(1) | Yes | Yes |
| Merge Sort | O(n log n) | O(n log n) | O(n log n) | O(n) | Yes | No |
| Quick Sort | O(n log n) | O(n²) | O(n log n) | O(log n)* | No | Yes |

*\*Quick Sort's space complexity refers to the recursion stack; it sorts in-place.*

---

## 1. Bubble Sort

### Concept
Bubble Sort repeatedly steps through the list, compares adjacent elements, and swaps them if they are in the wrong order. The pass through the list is repeated until the list is sorted. Larger elements "bubble up" to the end of the array.

### Complexity Analysis

- **Best Case:** O(n) — when the array is already sorted (with an optimization flag).
- **Average Case:** O(n²) — each element may need to be compared with every other element.
- **Worst Case:** O(n²) — when the array is sorted in reverse order.
- **Space:** O(1) — only a constant amount of extra memory is needed.
- **Stability:** Stable — equal elements retain their relative order.

### Code Example in C

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdbool.h>

void bubble_sort(int arr[], int n) {
    for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
        bool swapped = false;
        // Last i elements are already in place
        for (int j = 0; j < n - i - 1; j++) {
            if (arr[j] > arr[j + 1]) {
                // Swap adjacent elements
                int temp = arr[j];
                arr[j] = arr[j + 1];
                arr[j + 1] = temp;
                swapped = true;
            }
        }
        // If no swaps occurred, array is already sorted
        if (!swapped) break;
    }
}

